From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 22:36:15 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 16/17] SDL: on KMSDRM, an on-screen keyboard for the gamepad

A handheld without a keyboard could not type anywhere: world names,
settings, a sign, a book, the creative inventory's search. With the
gamepad driving the drawn cursor (KMSDRM), a click (A/Y) that lands on
an edit box that takes text now opens a keyboard over the screen, the
goodluckOS launcher's: a staggered QWERTY of five rows (digits, letters,
- ' ( ) . space), the D-pad moving between keys with the launcher's
repeat (350 ms, then every 60 ms).

While it is open the gamepad drives it instead of the cursor: the
button that clicks in menus types the key, the one that goes back
deletes (and closes the keyboard when nothing is left), START closes
it, SELECT closes it and gives the box back the text it had, L1/R1 move
the cursor in the text. Aa switches to capitals. What is typed reaches
the box as SDL's text input and keys do, through the user receiver and
then the focus, so every edit box works unchanged. It closes when the
box loses the focus or goes away (a formspec closing). It sits at the
bottom, as far from it as from the sides; over a box it covers, its top
line shows the text.

The labels of space, delete and ok and the line of hints come from the
client in the user's language (pt_BR included). The hints name the
buttons by position as goodluckOS maps them: B (bottom) types, A
(right) deletes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

